## Resolved Wording Interpretation -- N34-Q03 / D

Location:
draft-FIRST.json /questions/2/key/rationales/3/derivation.

The opening procedure says:
"Truncate negative exponents toward zero and keep the absolute fractional part".
Its explicit examples use -.6 -> 0+.4, -2.6 -> -2+.4,
-.41 -> 0+.59 and -3.41 -> -3+.59.

An initial provisional reading took "absolute fractional part" to mean the
absolute remainder AFTER truncation, which would be .6/.41. That reading is
not required by the actual lesson: its fractional part is explicitly
r=x-floor(x), already nonnegative. For the four inputs r is .4/.4/.59/.59.
The author's four explicit examples disambiguate its intended mistake:
retain those correct nonnegative fractional parts but use trunc(x) instead of
floor(x) for the integer part. This raises each negative exponent by1 and
generates exactly the displayed factor-ten-too-large entries.

Thus the actual stated examples do generate the distractor, and the procedure
is a coherent mistaken combination of truncated integer and floor remainder.
It must not be marked defective merely by imposing the alternative
truncation-remainder convention. The provisional REVISE interpretation was
resolved BEFORE either final artifact was frozen; it is not a final finding.
Option D remains false, the original independent evidence is unchanged, and
the correct set remains{A}. No author repair is required.

